00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.289 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  my first time on Mars was unforgettable  after touring every corner of the earth  living in post-apocalyptic Boston and  studying at a dozen universities I  thought I had seen it all but well on  the ground on the Red Planet driving my  rover through the whippin dust that  giddy feeling of wonder swept over me  once again of course I wasn't actually  in any of these places but both my brain  and my body believed that I was and at  the end of the day is there really any  difference I'm talking of course about  virtual reality virtual reality has been  called many things from a passing fad  like 3d television to the next big  computing platform on the level of  mobile phones by now many of us have  already had the chance to try out VR  once or twice and have already started  to make assumptions about the impact  that it may or may not have but  regardless of your stance if I ask you  to picture who's actually using VR today  most of us will come up with an image  like this someone someone isolated in  their living room waving their arms  furiously in the air in between sips of  silence and handfuls of Doritos there's  a ton of stigma about VR people think  it's for gamers or claim that it's  overhyped or fear that it's going to  have unintended consequences on humanity  and for the record I think these are all  legitimate concerns but looking beyond  them there is so much more to virtual  reality and the promise that it holds I  believe in a future where VR will be  used around the world to improve  people's lives and bring us closer  together but we're a long way away from  that right now which leads us to the  biggest open question facing it today  what is it really for  or perhaps more importantly what can it  be for my own obsession with VR lies at  the heart of this question in my quest  to answer it I try to enter VR every  single day for the past few years even  if just for a few minutes at a time in  that time I've seen some things I've met  people and made real connections I've  used it as a tool to improve learning  and education and I've even built new  worlds and shared those experiences with  other people but regardless of the  specific application there's one  fundamental concept that makes VR so  promising to me and all of its  enthusiasts it's its ability to create  presence presence is achieved when your  brain truly believes that you're no  longer just standing in your bedroom or  your living room but somewhere else  entirely what VR is and will be for is  anything that requires presence and  especially when presence is either  expensive or even impossible to achieve  consider this we spend a tremendous  amount of time transporting our physical  brains to places that they're not think  of multi-hour commutes or  flying around the world just to go to a  meeting or two why do we do this it's  because being face to face with someone  feeling present in the same room matters  video conferencing was heralded as the  technology that was going to end the  office as we know it but it never  delivered on that promise because it  couldn't achieve presence but what if we  could create VR experiences that made  you truly feel like you're in that  meeting with those people how would our  concept of the commute or work in  general change  well presence is foundational what's  even more exciting is what we can build  on top of it the research shows that  when we were present in VR it actually  makes us feel like things are happening  to us as opposed to us just watching  them happen to someone  and when we can truly believe that we  are somewhere or even someone else we've  just entered the most powerful vehicle  for empathy that we've ever created now  let's pause and reflect for a moment why  did you all come here today to watch low  key notes instead of just catching it on  YouTube later it certainly can't be  because of comfort I'm sure your couch  is much nicer than these chairs and  definitely standing on almost all  metrics the at-home experience is better  than life except for one we come to live  events like these because we want to  feel something to absorb the energy of  the speaker and the people around us to  laugh and to cry and to share those  feelings with the people sitting next to  us in these live experiences it's our  presence that powers empathy but we  can't always come to live events just  like we can't always fly around the  world to understand how people live  their lives so it's you to achieve  presence we usually rely on reading  stories or watching videos but it's one  thing to read a story about Italy or to  see a picture of Rome or to even watch a  movie like gladiator but it's an  entirely different thing to step into  Julius Caesar shoes and see the world as  he did the research supports the fact  that when we have experiences even if  they're just simulated ones it makes us  much better connect with and understand  that content up to four times more than  if we were just to read about it or  watch it so well TV gives us a window to  peer into another world VR lets us step  into that world and truly immerse  ourselves in it it's an entirely new  medium for empathetic storytelling but  what stories can we tell consider clouds  over Sidra a VR experience built in  partnership with the United Nations and  presented at the World Economic Forum  the mission was to take global leaders  and help them better understand  what life is like for a young girl  living in a Syrian refugee camp and  inspire them to start making a  difference or consider melting ice a vr  documentary featuring al gore that  transports people to the remote parts of  the world that are most affected by  climate change to give them a vivid  understanding of the real impact that  it's already having VR is also being  used to combat domestic violence in a  recent study researchers have taken men  who have committed these acts and put  them into VR except in the body of a  woman  the men are then subjected to simulated  domestic violence and it's been proven  that it actually makes them more  empathetic to the others around them and  will hopefully prevent future incidents  these are just a few of the examples  that are possible now and every day  we're learning more about how we can use  VR as a tool to reimagine empathy well  I'm clearly just a little bit obsessed  about VR I don't think that it's me or  even anyone in this room who stands the  most to gain from it VR is going to  unlock experiences for the vast majority  of people in this world who don't have  access to them right now consider the  moment when anyone with a headset can  feel like they're sitting in a classroom  at a top university listening to  inspiring educators and having real  social interactions with the people next  to them that's the moment when we've  made world-class education available to  anyone the release of cheap but  high-quality VR headsets in the coming  years will make it possible for everyone  not just to feel greater empathy but to  have experiences that were previously  impossible for them to have so  regardless of the opinion about VR you  walked in with here today I encourage  you to broaden your perspective on what  it can and will be for much like how  black-and-white television or the first  mobile phone only hinted at what was to  come  VR is still very much in its infancy it  will take time but it's a future worth  building towards and in that future  everyone will have the ability to feel  present anywhere with anyone at anytime  and the human race will be much more  connected because of it thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

